--- body ---
## Context

Raid 80974c (2026-10-30) twice put a "continue?" question to the author on
PR #2054 while a built fix sat on a branch unreviewed. Gaze ran only after the
answer. Both times the gaze result was the answer: a further blocker meant
park, a clean result meant gate. The author: "instead of waiting, it could have
solved the question."

`rules/workflow.md` § Escalation protocol step 2 builds reversible options on
their own branch within 5 minutes but does not review them; gaze already takes
a `branch:<name>` unit with no merge request.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] The escalation protocol makes the review of a built option part of building it: gaze on the option's branch runs while the question is pending, and the question to the author carries the result (or is dropped when the result settles it)
- [ ] A review on the option branch carries over to the PR when its reviewed head is the head that lands (bookkeeping delta at most), otherwise it is discarded; one gaze per request at a time still holds
- [ ] `skills/raid/references/fix-and-regate.md` points at the rule instead of restating it

## Plan (raid 0da24b; author ruling 2026-10-30, option (b): carry-over in code)

Premise holds at origin/main 4269e12e. `review_notes.find_unit` (~765) matches
only `unit == "pr:<n>"`; the two openers of a PR review, `gaze_setup.py`
(~193, `resolve_unit(...) or open_review(..., unit=...)`) and
`attribution_record.py` (~397, writer line before any review), mint a fresh
review, so `gate_state(review_id, head)` (~882) sees no answer and says
"no completed review" even when gaze already answered `branch:<name>` on the
very head the PR carries.

Actions:
1. Red test first (tests/test_review_notes.py or its nearest sibling): gaze-record
   an answer on `branch:x` at head H, open `pr:N` for branch x at H through the
   same call the openers use, assert `gate_state` for the pr:N review at H is
   `ready`. Second case, same file: the branch review's answer is on an older
   head H0 and the PR head adds code (not bookkeeping) → no adoption, a fresh
   review, gate state not ready. Show the first red for the targeted reason.
2. Code, one choke point: `review_notes.open_review` with `unit="pr:<n>"`
   adopts the latest `find(cwd, branch)` review when its latest answer's head
   equals the PR head, or the PR head adds only bookkeeping to it
   (`_bookkeeping_since`); otherwise it opens a fresh review (the branch review
   is left as it is, discarded for this PR). Adoption = the review keeps its id
   and gains the `pr:<n>` request (request() already carries `unit` forward), so
   `find_unit` finds it and its answers, writer lines and rounds come along.
   The executor may pick another mechanism if this one breaks an invariant
   (e.g. `find` must keep skipping PR reviews for later branch reuse); say why.
3. Docs, stated once: the carry-over rule goes in `skills/merge/SKILL.md`
   § Gating tiers (a `branch:<name>` review whose reviewed head is the head that
   lands, bookkeeping delta at most, is the PR's review; otherwise discarded;
   one gaze per request at a time still holds). Cite it, do not restate it, from
   `rules/workflow.md` (the one-gaze-per-request clause ~:150) and
   `skills/raid/references/fix-and-regate.md`.
4. Rewrite `rules/workflow.md` § Escalation protocol step 2 (~105-108): an
   option is built when gaze (`branch:<name>`) has run on it; the question to the
   author carries the result, or is dropped when the result settles it; the
   5-minute cap is on building only; gaze the recommended option (plus a likely
   author pick), not every option; "no outward act" exempts gaze's notes sync
   (`gaze_close.py` pushes notes, ~283). Plain git wording: "its own worktree
   and branch", never "fork" in the subagent sense (Codex and Pi are
   first-class). Keep the section's length about the same.
5. No new guards and no tests about tests. Gate: `make check` (Python changed).

Antipattern scan: the red test exercises the opener path and `gate_state`, so a
wrong adoption rule (wrong head, code delta adopted) fails case 2; not
tautological. YAGNI: no new CLI verb, no new entry kind.

Review: this is a gate-path change, so its gaze includes a cross-family seat.
